// ★★★ NOTHING HERE WAS TYPED FROM MEMORY.
//
//  · The BIT ALLOCATION tables come from ETSI TS 103 466 (the DAB audio spec) tables 4, 5 and 6,
//    machine-parsed out of the PDF. The parser checked its own work against the spec's stated
//    sums: "If sblimit is equal to 27, the sum of nbal is equal to 88 … equal to 26" for the
//    8-subband table. Both matched.
//
//  · The SYNTHESIS WINDOW is not in any spec I could obtain — TS 103 466 defers to ISO/IEC
//    11172-3, which is not free. So it was MEASURED instead: a hand-built MP2 frame carrying a
//    single non-zero subband sample was decoded by a known-good decoder, and with one sample
//    active the ISO synthesis reduces to exactly one term per output,
//        out_g[n] = A · cos((16+i)π/64) · D[32g + n],
//    so every coefficient falls straight out of the impulse response. Eight coefficients sit
//    where that cosine vanishes and were filled from the window's own symmetry
//    (D[512-i] = -D[i], or +D[i] when i is a multiple of 64) — which was first VERIFIED on the
//    500-odd coefficients where both halves had been measured independently.
//  ★ Two facts confirm the result against published values: the peak is 1.144989, and
//    D[1..6] = -0.000015259. Both match the ISO table.
